WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.500
&gt;&gt;&gt; First let me say public office is really
important to have more women and people of

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:12.100
color in state legislatures, in local city
councils, and also at the federal level because

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.930
when you get women and people of color in
these state houses, they make new policies

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:18.930
for society.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.630
They change society.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.070
&gt;&gt; That is philanthropist Melinda French Gates.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.439
She's of course a cofounder of the bill and
Melinda Gates foundation.

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:30.970
That foundation has given away more than $70
billion in grants.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:36.320
A long-time advocate for women and girls,
she is now focused on getting more women elected

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.650
to public office.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:43.270
French Gates explains why in a recent op-ed,
quote, only one in three state legislatures

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.270
in the U.S.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.680
Is a woman, and at the federal level it's
closer to one in four.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.700
There is evidence that women govern differently,
working more collaboratively across party

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.630
lines and introducing legislation on issues
that have

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:56.630
[6:48:54 AM]

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.630
legislation on issues that have historically
gone unaddressed.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:03.760
I spoke with her about those efforts, also
her concerns with ai and life after her very

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.280
public divorce with Bill Gates.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:18.010
&gt;&gt; So you start this op-ed in time writing
in 1976 Annabelle Clemente Brian ran on the

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.920
slogan a woman's place is in the house and
the senate too.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.360
Why is so much of your effort and your money
going to getting more women elected?

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.490
&gt;&gt; Well, because I believe women should have
their full power and influence in the United

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:38.869
States, and in 2019 I made a billion dollars
commitment to ensure that really starts to

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:44.330
happen more, with more momentum, and I'm just
seeing that we aren't there yet.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:51.340
Down, too often we have decisions being made
for women, not by women, and as she said and

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:57.259
also as Ruth Bader Ginsburg said, I believe
women should be every place that decisions

00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:58.259
are being made, and that's just --

00:01:58.259 --> 00:01:59.259
[6:49:54 AM]

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:00.630
being made, and that's just -- we're just
not there yet as a country.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.430
&gt;&gt; What's interesting about your effort is
you're really keenly focused on state legislatures,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.430
why?

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:11.739
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm focused on making sure that women
have their rightful place at all levels of

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:15.280
governing, but state legislators are particularly
important.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.599
There are 7,000 seats at the legislative level
at states.

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:26.180
They control $2 trillion in resources, and
they make really important policies and laws

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.180
that affect everyone in their state.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:33.680
So really focusing there where there's so
many seats, but also for those women who do

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.230
want to go on and be in the halls of congress
it's a great training ground.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.209
&gt;&gt; Democrats and Republicans you want to help
across parties?

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:43.209
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:49.480
We need our government to represent all of
us, and we have different points of view depending

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.790
on what state you're in, depending on your
political leanings.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.480
So absolutely, both sides of the aisle.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.480
[6:50:54 AM]

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.480
aisle.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.170
&gt;&gt; Former Republican congresswoman Liz Cheney
was quite blunt speaking in New York this

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.420
week, here's what she said.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:11.040
&gt;&gt; What we've done in our politics is create
a situation where we're electing idiots.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:19.000
[ Laughter ]
[ Applause ] And so I don't look at it through

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.430
the lens of like, you know, is this what I
should do or what I shouldn't do.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.930
I look at it through the lens of how do we
elect serious people, and I think electing

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.150
serious people can't be partisan.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.150
&gt;&gt; I'm wondering if you agree, if you think
that electing more women helps solve that

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.319
problem?

00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:41.950
&gt;&gt; I think we need to make sure that everyone
in this country's voice is represented, so

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.580
whether that's a female, whether that's a
male.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.140
We have to realize we just aren't there yet.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.020
One this three state legislators are women.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:54.269
One in four are women in congress, and yet,
we're 50% of the population.

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:55.269
How can we have 16 million black

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:56.269
[6:51:54 AM]

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:03.230
How can we have 16 million black women in
this country but zero black senators?

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:08.540
So we know that point of view isn't being
represented, and we need to do more to make

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.080
sure that women are represented.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.470
&gt;&gt; I obviously couldn't help notice you wrote
this, this was published really at the one-year

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.970
mark of roe versus wade being overturned.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.099
Was that a coincidence?

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:22.099
&gt;&gt; Not at all a coincidence.

00:04:22.099 --> 00:04:28.509
I mean to have a law on the books for almost
50 years around women's reproductive rights

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:35.870
and then have it rolled back, and when you
really go and talk to and do the research

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.820
about what do Americans believe, they believe
that law should be in place.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.570
So to me it was a decision, again, where a
decision made for women, not by women.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:50.470
We should never roll back a law like that
that has to do with women's health.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:54.160
That's a very private, hard, emotional decision.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.949
The government shouldn't be involved and hasn't
been involved in 50 years, so why would we

00:04:57.949 --> 00:04:58.949
change that?

00:04:58.949 --> 00:04:59.949
[6:52:55 AM]

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:00.949
would we change that?

00:05:00.949 --> 00:05:04.371
&gt;&gt; This reminded me of something you said
a long time ago when I spoke to you.

00:05:04.371 --> 00:05:10.440
You said that your mother taught you set your
own agenda or someone else will.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:16.099
Does all of this really stem from that, set
your own agenda or someone else will.

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:22.910
&gt;&gt; Yes, because the agenda being set by the
United States is being set by a group of people

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:27.220
that doesn't represent all of us, and what
we know is that women when they come into

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.760
halls of power, they introduce new pieces
of legislation that have historically not

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.910
been there because they have a different lens
on society, and so this is about making sure

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.130
we set the agenda for all the American people.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:45.660
Our democracy should represent us, and it
does not today.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:51.240
&gt;&gt; So in all of my years of interviewing you,
you have never endorsed publicly, am I right,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.099
a candidate?

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:54.449
&gt;&gt; I think that's right.

00:05:54.449 --> 00:05:57.400
&gt;&gt; Does this -- does that change now, Melinda?

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.780
&gt;&gt; No, I have given personal resources and
continue to give

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:02.780
[6:53:59 AM]

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.150
resources and continue to give personal resources
to many candidates on both sides of the aisle,

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.139
but I don't endorse a specific person.

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:11.410
&gt;&gt; Can I ask you why, if you're really putting
a lot more money and energy behind politics

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:15.010
and getting women elected and behind causes
that you're passionate about, roe versus wade

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:20.000
being overturned as an example, why not publicly
endorse if there are candidates in this next

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.809
cycle that you think are necessary in office?

00:06:23.809 --> 00:06:27.540
&gt;&gt; Because I vote in any election on both
sides of the aisle.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.880
Sometimes I vote Republican, sometimes democratic,
I'm a very independent voter, and I don't

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.539
want to be pegged as one or the other.

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:39.949
I think that the best policy is made when
we reach across the aisle, and so I think

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:43.220
if I come out for a particular candidate,
they're going to say, oh, she always supports

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:47.440
Republicans or she always supports a Democrat,
and that's just not true.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.010
&gt;&gt; So many people are focused on artificial
intelligence now, and you've been warning

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:57.120
for years not just about what is ahead with
ai but about bias built into ai, and you've

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.261
equated it to the bias built into the constitution,
right?

00:06:59.261 --> 00:07:00.630
Who was the constitution written

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:01.630
[6:54:59 AM]

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.050
Who was the constitution written by.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:09.130
How nervous are you that that could be playing
out again when it comes to ai?

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:14.750
&gt;&gt; I'm very nervous because we don't have
enough women, again, who are computer scientists

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:21.500
and who have can expertise in artificial intelligence,
and without that, we will bake bias into the

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:22.500
system.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.780
Again, the system needs to take all people's
points of view and see society and quite frankly,

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.410
see the world writ large as it is.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:35.690
When you have women at any of these places,
when you're creating something, when you're

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.889
making this decision, when you're setting
a law, you're bringing that perspective of

00:07:40.889 --> 00:07:44.960
society that is just so vitally important.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.800
&gt;&gt; You have, Melinda, described this moment
in your life as incredibly joyful.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.889
So many people in America and around the world
have gone through painful divorces.

00:07:54.889 --> 00:08:00.279
Can you talk about a little bit about your
journey in finding this joy?

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:02.360
&gt;&gt; I think, you know, you have to look for
it every day.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.360
[6:56:00 AM]

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.360
to look for it every day.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.220
Even when you go through a painful time, which
I did for several years, you gather these

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:13.539
moments of joy during the day and then you
reflect on them at the end of the week.

00:08:13.539 --> 00:08:17.720
And so I happen to be in a situation now in
life where I have a granddaughter.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.919
My three children are out of the house now,
and so I just really surround myself with

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:27.789
family and friends who bring me a lot of energy
and joy so when I do go out in the world and

00:08:27.789 --> 00:08:33.900
do this kind of work or I travel to places
that are very difficult, I can lean into that

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:40.140
joy and lean into that network of family and
friends that support us.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.700
&gt;&gt; I think, poppy, we talked about this a
little yesterday after the interview and I'll

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:49.089
acknowledge candidly I went back and was watching
the feed in our system just because I was

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:53.100
very interested in the actual interview itself,
but you mentioned it yesterday, and I want

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.650
to follow up on it because your perspective
on the non-endorsements, particularly when

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:03.250
you take a very straightforward line on roe
versus wade, and I've seen a lot of people

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.480
with a lot of money in politics try and strike
this

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.480
[6:57:02 AM]

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.800
politics try and strike this middle ground
and within a year or two, their grand efforts

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.790
have completely faded away.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:12.339
What was your --
&gt;&gt; Why doesn't she endorse?

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:18.290
I think you heard her say there because she's
not just a Democrat, not just a Republican,

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.590
she's an independent, but you could still
endorse certain candidates.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.540
I think she doesn't want to alienate people.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:30.120
I think she believes that she can make change
without publicly saying who, not just with

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.329
money but with resources, with the writing
that she does.

00:09:33.329 --> 00:09:34.970
But it was really interesting.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:40.010
It's just been fascinating over the last decade
to watch her evolution in terms of such supportive

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:47.090
women and girls as you heard the timing of
this was not a coincidence at all.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.860
I think you're going to see and hear a lot
more from Melinda Gates on this front.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:10:01.149
Maybe one day she will endorse.

